PR #9  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Matrix multiply-accumulate instructions must hardwire vm=1; vm=0 is reserved. An implementation silently accepting vm=0 would apply undefined element-level masking to 2D tile accumulations, producing implementation-specific partial results and silent data corruption in security-critical matrix computations.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:1-119  (top-level: RISCV_ISA_EXT_* enum)
      The enum currently has no ZVMM or ZVVFMM entries (max is ZICFISS at 105, MAX=128). The enum itself is just ID allocation — no security logic lives here. The vm=1 enforcement belongs in cpufeature.c validation callbacks, not in the enum definition. When ZVMM is added, the only risk here is exhausting the 128-slot bitmap if too many extensions are added between ZICFISS (105) and MAX (128). Currently 22 slots remain, which is ample.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:460-593  riscv_isa_ext (extension validation data array)
      ZVFBFWMA (the closest existing analogue to ZVMM) has an entry at line 561 with riscv_ext_zvfbfwma_validate, which only checks ZFBFMIN+ZVFBFMIN ISA dependencies — it does NOT validate vm=1 hardwiring. If ZVMM/ZVVFMM are added following this pattern, they will be accepted on any hardware that advertises the ISA string without checking whether the implementation actually hardwires vm=1. The validation callback infrastructure has no mechanism for probing instruction-level behavior; it only checks ISA bitmap dependencies. This is the root of the semantic gap: the kernel currently has no way to programmatically verify that hardware enforces vm=1.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:173-196  do_trap_insn_illegal
      This handler is structurally correct for the vm=0 scenario: if hardware raises EXC_ILLEGAL_INSN for vm=0 matrix instructions, user-mode gets SIGILL (via failed riscv_v_first_use_handler fallthrough) and kernel-mode gets SIGILL unconditionally. The handler itself needs no change. The risk is external to this function: if hardware silently accepts vm=0 without trapping, this fault handler is never invoked, and the violation goes undetected. The handler's correctness is contingent on hardware correctly raising the trap.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/vector.c:81-112  insn_is_vector
      This classifier only matches RVV_OPCODE_VECTOR (0x57), vector load/store (0x07/0x27), and V CSR range. Matrix multiply-accumulate instructions use a different major opcode (RVV_OPCODE_VECTOR=0x57 is the standard V opcode; matrix instructions use a reserved encoding space), so this function correctly returns false for them today. However, the function name 'insn_is_vector' semantically suggests it covers all vector-ISA-family instructions, which matrix instructions are part of. A future developer adding ZVMM support might extend this function to also match the matrix opcode, which would route matrix instructions into the V lazy-enable path without adding vm=0 rejection logic — creating a path where vm=0 silently executes with V context allocated.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/vector.c:185-228  riscv_v_first_use_handler
