PR #9  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Matrix multiply-accumulate instructions must hardwire vm=1; vm=0 is reserved. An implementation silently accepting vm=0 would apply undefined element-level masking to 2D tile accumulations, producing implementation-specific partial results and silent data corruption in security-critical matrix computations.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:148-175  sbi_illegal_insn_handler
      OP-V (opcode 0x57) dispatches to index 21 (truly_illegal_insn) via the illegal_insn_table lookup at line 174. On a hart that does NOT implement Zvmm, a vm=0 macc correctly traps as illegal and reaches this handler for S-mode redirect. On a Zvmm-capable hart that executes vm=0 silently in hardware (pre-clarification RTL), this handler is never invoked — the reserved encoding executes without M-mode visibility. The correctness of the entire M-mode/S-mode trap chain depends on the unverified invariant that 'hardware traps vm=0 matrix macc as illegal instruction.'
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:23-34  truly_illegal_insn
      Correctly constructs trap_info with cause=CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION and tval=raw_insn, then calls sbi_trap_redirect(). The tval carries the full instruction encoding, so S-mode can inspect the vm bit to distinguish vm=0 vs vm=1 — but only when hardware actually delivers the trap. No defect in this function itself.
